   "site": "Upper trunk (C5, C6) at Erb’s point",
   "cause": "A fall on the shoulder that forces the head and shoulder apart, or traction on the head during a difficult birth.",
   "sees": "‘Waiter’s tip’: the arm hangs by the side, adducted and medially rotated, with the elbow extended, the forearm pronated and the wrist flexed.",
   "moves": "Abduction and lateral rotation of the shoulder, flexion of the elbow and supination of the forearm.",
   "reflex": "Biceps and supinator (brachioradialis) jerks lost.",
   "signs": "Serratus anterior and the rhomboids still work, because their nerves leave the roots above the trunk. If they are weak too, suspect the roots have been torn from the spinal cord."
  },
